::GEO_U05_L07_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L07_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.8<br><br>Question:<br>Circle problem 1 compares a circle of radius 6 to a circle of radius 6. Which transformation proves similarity? {
=A. A dilation with scale factor 2, possibly after a rigid motion#A dilation maps one radius length to the other, and a rigid motion can align centers.
~B. A translation only#A translation changes location but not radius.
~C. A reflection only#A reflection preserves size and cannot change radius.
~D. No transformation is possible#A dilation gives a valid similarity transformation.
}

::GEO_U05_L07_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L07_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.5<br><br>Question:<br>Circle problem 2 has an inscribed angle intercepting a 92&deg; arc. What is the angle measure? {
~A. 92&deg;#That is the central angle relationship, not the inscribed angle relationship.
=B. 46&deg;#An inscribed angle measures half its intercepted arc.
~C. 184&deg;#This doubles instead of halves the arc.
~D. Cannot be determined#The intercepted arc measure is enough information.
}

::GEO_U05_L07_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L07_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.6<br><br>Question:<br>Circle problem 3 shows a cyclic quadrilateral with one angle 84&deg;. What is the opposite angle? {
~A. 84&deg;#Opposite angles are not necessarily congruent.
~B. 104&deg;#This does not use the 180&deg; sum.
=C. 96&deg;#Opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary.
~D. 90&deg;#A right angle is not guaranteed.
}

::GEO_U05_L07_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L07_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Circle problem 4 has a tangent length t and a secant with external segment 7 and whole length 28. What is t? {
~A. 35#Adding lengths does not use the tangent-secant theorem.
~B. 196#This is t<sup>2</sup>, not t.
~C. Cannot be determined#The tangent-secant relationship gives enough information.
=D. 14#Use t<sup>2</sup> = external &times; whole, so t = 14.
}

::GEO_U05_L07_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L07_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.1<br><br>Question:<br>Construction problem 5 asks for a circumscribed circle of a triangle. Which construction locates its center? {
=A. Perpendicular bisectors of the triangle sides#The circumcenter is the intersection of perpendicular bisectors.
~B. Angle bisectors of the triangle#Angle bisectors locate the incenter.
~C. A single median#One median does not locate the circumcenter.
~D. A tangent from one vertex#A tangent construction does not locate the circumscribed-circle center.
}

::GEO_U05_L07_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L07_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Construction problem 6 asks for a regular hexagon inscribed in a circle. What supports the equal side construction? {
~A. Using the diameter as each side#The diameter is too long to be each side.
=B. Stepping the radius around the circle creates six equal chords.#A regular hexagon inscribed in a circle has side length equal to the radius.
~C. Choosing any six points#The vertices must be equally spaced.
~D. Drawing six tangents#Tangents are outside contact lines, not inscribed sides.
}

::GEO_U05_L07_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L07_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Construction problem 7 draws a tangent from an external point. What must be true at the point of tangency? {
~A. The tangent passes through the center.#A line through the center is not tangent at the circle.
~B. The tangent intersects the circle twice.#That describes a secant.
=C. The tangent is perpendicular to the radius.#A tangent to a circle is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency.
~D. The tangent equals the diameter.#Tangent length is not defined by matching the diameter.
}

::GEO_U05_L07_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L07_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.7<br><br>Question:<br>Measurement problem 8 has radius 8 and central angle 80&deg;. Which expression gives sector area? {
~A. <sup>80</sup>&frasl;<sub>360</sub>(2&pi;8)#This is arc length, not sector area.
~B. 2&pi;8#This is full circumference.
~C. &pi;8<sup>2</sup>#This is full circle area, not the sector area unless the angle is 360&deg;.
=D. <sup>80</sup>&frasl;<sub>360</sub>(&pi;8<sup>2</sup>)#Sector area is the central-angle fraction of circle area.
}

